> inverse 3 mod 256
3^-1 = 171 mod 256
  how: extended Euclid: 171*3 - 2*256 = 1
  check: 3 * 171 = 513 = 2*256 + 1
> inverse 6 mod 15
6 has no inverse mod 15: gcd(6, 15) = 3 > 1
  6 * 5 = 30 = 0 mod 15, so 6 is a zero divisor; a*x = 1 would give 5 = 5 * 6 * x = 0
  the units mod 15 are the residues coprime to 15 (phi(15) = 8 of them)
> inverse 17 mod 3120
17^-1 = 2753 mod 3120 (0xac1)
  how: extended Euclid: 2753*17 - 15*3120 = 1
  check: 17 * 2753 = 46801 = 15*3120 + 1
> inverse 3 mod 2^32
3^-1 = 0xaaaaaaab mod 2^32 (2863311531)
  how: Newton's iteration y = y*(2 - a*y), from y = a (right to 3 bits: a*a = 1 mod 8 for odd a); each step doubles the correct bits: 4 steps
  check: 0x3 * 0xaaaaaaab = 1 mod 2^32 (the product's low 32 bits are 0x1)
  as u32 arithmetic: x * 0xaaaaaaab undoes x * 0x3 (multiplying by an odd constant is a bijection of u32), and x * 0xaaaaaaab = x / 3 exactly when 3 divides x
  check in ucas's u32 evaluator: x * 0x3 * 0xaaaaaaab = x for the 256 smallest and largest x and 65536 random x (66048 inputs)
> inverse 7 mod 2^64
7^-1 = 0x6db6db6db6db6db7 mod 2^64 (7905747460161236407)
  how: Newton's iteration y = y*(2 - a*y), from y = a (right to 3 bits: a*a = 1 mod 8 for odd a); each step doubles the correct bits: 5 steps
  check: 0x7 * 0x6db6db6db6db6db7 = 1 mod 2^64 (the product's low 64 bits are 0x1)
  as u64 arithmetic: x * 0x6db6db6db6db6db7 undoes x * 0x7 (multiplying by an odd constant is a bijection of u64), and x * 0x6db6db6db6db6db7 = x / 7 exactly when 7 divides x
  check in ucas's u64 evaluator: x * 0x7 * 0x6db6db6db6db6db7 = x for the 256 smallest and largest x and 65536 random x (66048 inputs)
> inverse 6 mod 2^8
0x6 has no inverse mod 2^8: it is even (gcd = 2^1), and 0x6 * 0x80 = 0 mod 2^8
  as u8 arithmetic: multiplying by an even constant loses the top bit, so it cannot be undone
> order 2 mod 101
ord(2) = 100 mod 101
  how: the order divides lambda(101) = 100; 2^100 = 1 and 2^(100/2) = 100, 2^(100/5) = 95
  2 is a primitive root mod 101 (its order is phi(101) = 100)
  additive order: 101
  check: stepping through the powers, 2^k = 1 first at k = 100
> order 3 mod 2^64
ord(0x3) = 2^62 mod 2^64
  how: the units mod 2^64 form C2 x C2^62, so the order is a power of two: square until the result is 1 (62 squarings)
  as u64 arithmetic: x * 0x3^(2^62) = x, so multiplying by 0x3 2^62 times is the identity
> phi 100
phi(100) = 40
  how: 100 = 2^2 * 5^2; phi = product of p^(e-1) (p - 1): 2^1*1 * 5^1*4
  the order of the unit group (Z/100)*; lambda(100) = 20 is its exponent
  check: counted the k in 1..100 with gcd(k, 100) = 1: 40
> carmichael 561
lambda(561) = 80 (Carmichael: the least m with a^m = 1 for every unit a)
  how: 561 = 3 * 11 * 17; lambda is the lcm of 2 (3^1), 10 (11^1), 16 (17^1)
  lambda(2^e) = 2^(e-2) for e >= 3; lambda(p^e) = phi(p^e) otherwise; phi(561) = 320
  check: the lcm of the orders of all units, by stepping through their powers: 80
> factorint 600851475143
600851475143 = 71 * 839 * 1471 * 6857 (not prime)
  how: trial division by the primes below 50, then Pollard-Brent rho; primality by Miller-Rabin with the first 12 prime bases (deterministic below 2^64)
  check: the product is 600851475143; each factor is prime
> ring Z/12
Z/12: the integers mod 12 = 2^2 * 3
  12 elements, characteristic 12
  a field: no; an integral domain: no (12 is not prime: 2 * 6 = 0 with both nonzero)
  CRT: Z/12 = Z/4 x Z/3 (a -> (a mod 4, a mod 3))
  units: phi(12) = 4: 1, 5, 7, 11
  unit group: C2 x C2 (order 4, exponent lambda(12) = 2); not cyclic, so there is no primitive root
  zero divisors: 7 nonzero (every nonzero non-unit of a finite ring): 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 9, 10
  nilpotents: the multiples of rad(12) = 6, 2 of them: 0, 6
  idempotents: 2^2 (e = 0 or 1 mod each prime power): 0, 1, 4, 9
  ideals: (d) for each d | 12 (6 of them; Z/12 is a principal ideal ring): (1) (2) (3) (4) (6) (0)
  maximal = prime ideals: (2), (3) (quotients Z/p, fields)
  check: by brute force over the 12 elements: 4 units, 7 zero divisors, 2 nilpotents, 4 idempotents
> ring Z/12 units
  units: phi(12) = 4: 1, 5, 7, 11
  unit group: C2 x C2 (order 4, exponent lambda(12) = 2); not cyclic, so there is no primitive root
> ring Z/12 zero divisors
  zero divisors: 7 nonzero (every nonzero non-unit of a finite ring): 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 9, 10
> ring Z/30 idempotents
  idempotents: 2^3 (e = 0 or 1 mod each prime power): 0, 1, 6, 10, 15, 16, 21, 25
> ring Z/7
Z/7: the integers mod 7
  7 elements, characteristic 7
  a field: yes (7 is prime: every nonzero a has an inverse, a^(7-2)); so also an integral domain
  units: phi(7) = 6: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6
  unit group: C6 (order 6, exponent lambda(7) = 6); cyclic, generated by the primitive root 3
  zero divisors: none besides 0
  nilpotents: the multiples of rad(7) = 7, 1 of them: 0
  idempotents: 2^1 (e = 0 or 1 mod each prime power): 0, 1
  ideals: (d) for each d | 7 (2 of them; Z/7 is a principal ideal ring): (1) (0)
  maximal = prime ideals: (7) (quotients Z/p, fields)
  check: by brute force over the 7 elements: 6 units, 0 zero divisors, 1 nilpotents, 2 idempotents
> ring Z/12 4
4 in Z/12
  not a unit: gcd(4, 12) = 4; a zero divisor: 4 * 3 = 0
  additive order 3
  idempotent: 4^2 = 4
  a square: 2^2, 4^2, 8^2, 10^2 = 4
> ring Zmod(8) 3
3 in Z/8
  a unit: inverse 3 (3 * 3 = 9 = 1 mod 8)
  multiplicative order 2
  additive order 8
  not a square mod 8
> congruence 3x = 4 mod 7
  3x = 4 mod 7: 3x = 4: x = 4 * 3^-1 = 4 * 5 = 6
x = 6 (mod 7)
  check: the solution substituted into the congruence: it holds; brute force over 0..6: 1 solution
> congruence 6x = 4 mod 10
  6x = 4 mod 10: 6x = 4: gcd(6, 10) = 2 divides 4: 3x = 2 mod 5, x = 4 (2 solutions mod 10)
x = 4 (mod 5)
  that is, mod 10: x = 4, 9
  check: the solution substituted into the congruence: it holds; brute force over 0..4: 1 solution
> crt x = 2 mod 3, x = 3 mod 5
the system
  x = 2 mod 3: x = 2
    x = 2 (mod 3)
  x = 3 mod 5: x = 3
    x = 3 (mod 5)
x = 8 (mod 15)
  how: the Chinese remainder theorem, pairwise (moduli that share factors must agree mod their gcd)
  check: the solution substituted into every congruence: it holds; brute force over 0..14: 1 solution
> crt x = 2 mod 4, x = 3 mod 6
the system
  x = 2 mod 4: x = 2
    x = 2 (mod 4)
  x = 3 mod 6: x = 3
    x = 3 (mod 6)
no solution: the congruences contradict each other (CRT: the residues differ mod the gcd of the moduli)
  check: brute force over 0..11: 0 solutions
> congruence x^2 + x + 1 = 0 mod 7
  x^2 + x + 1 = 0 mod 7: degree 2: tried every x mod 7
x = 2, 4 (mod 7)
  check: each solution substituted into the congruence: it holds; brute force over 0..6: 2 solutions
> legendre 2 7
(2/7) = 1
  a quadratic residue: x^2 = a has a solution
  (4^2 = 2 mod 7)
  how: quadratic reciprocity (the binary Jacobi algorithm)
  check: Euler's criterion 2^((7-1)/2) = 1 mod 7
> jacobi 2 15
(2/15) = 1
  how: quadratic reciprocity; the product of the Legendre symbols over 15 = 3 * 5
  15 is composite: (a/n) = 1 does not make a a square mod n; here 2 is not a square mod 15
> sqrtmod 2 7
x^2 = 2 mod 7: x = 3, 4
  how: p = 3 mod 4: x = a^((p+1)/4)
  check: 3^2 = 2, 4^2 = 2 (mod 7); brute force over 0..6: 2 solutions
> sqrt 4 mod 15
x^2 = 4 mod 15: x = 2, 7, 8, 13
  how: 15 = 3 * 5: roots mod each prime (Tonelli-Shanks), lifted to the prime powers (Hensel), combined by CRT
  check: 2^2 = 4, 7^2 = 4, 8^2 = 4, 13^2 = 4 (mod 15); brute force over 0..14: 4 solutions
> sqrtmod 5 mod 2^61 - 1
x^2 = 5 mod 2305843009213693951: x = 659791110852991619, 1646051898360702332
  how: p = 3 mod 4: x = a^((p+1)/4)
  check: 659791110852991619^2 = 5, 1646051898360702332^2 = 5 (mod 2305843009213693951)
> qr 11
the squares mod 11: 6 of 11 residues
  0, 1, 3, 4, 5, 9
  11 is prime: the nonzero squares are (p-1)/2 = 5, the a with (a/p) = 1
  how: x^2 mod 11 for every x
> primroot 11
the smallest primitive root mod 11 is 2
  how: (Z/11)* is cyclic of order phi = 10 = 2 * 5; g generates it iff g^(10/p) != 1 for each prime p | 10
  phi(10) = 4 primitive roots: 2 6 7 8
  check: the powers 2^0 .. 2^9 are 10 distinct units
> primroot 12
there is no primitive root mod 12: the unit group has exponent lambda(12) = 2 < phi(12) = 4, so it is not cyclic
  primitive roots exist only mod 1, 2, 4, p^k and 2p^k (p an odd prime)
> primroot 2 mod 11
yes: 2 is a primitive root mod 11 (its order is 10, phi(11) = 10)
  how: 2^(10/2) = 10, 2^(10/5) = 4 (a primitive root has none of these equal to 1)
  check: the powers 2^0 .. 2^9 are 10 distinct units
> dlog 3 base 2 mod 11
2^x = 3 in Z/11: x = 8 (mod 10, the order of 2)
  how: Pohlig-Hellman over 10 = 2 * 5, baby-step giant-step for each prime
  check: 2^8 = 3
> dlog 5 base 2 mod 1000003
2^x = 5 in Z/1000003: x = 292379 (mod 1000002, the order of 2)
  how: Pohlig-Hellman over 1000002 = 2 * 3 * 166667, baby-step giant-step for each prime
  check: 2^292379 = 5
> magic 7 u32
x / 7 for unsigned x: u32
  m = ceil(2^35 / 7) = 4908534053 (0x124924925, 33 bits), shift s = 3
  q = floor(x * m / 2^35)
  proof (Granlund-Montgomery): m*7 = 2^35 + e with e = 3 <= 2^3.  Write x = q*7 + r, 0 <= r < 7.  Then x*m/2^35 = x/7 + x*e/(7*2^35), and x*e/2^35 < 1 because |x| < 2^32, so the extra term is below 1/7: it cannot carry r/7 <= 6/7 past the next integer
  m has 33 bits, one more than u32: the usual sequence uses m - 2^32 = 0x24924925 and an add that cannot overflow:
    C:    t = mulhi(x, 0x24924925); q = (t + ((x - t) >> 1)) >> 2;
    in ucas: (u32((u64(x) * 0x24924925) >> 32) + ((x - u32((u64(x) * 0x24924925) >> 32)) >> 1)) >> 2
  check: the ucas form above, run by ucas's evaluator in u32, equals x / 7 for the 256 smallest and largest x and 65536 random x (66048 inputs)
  check: the sequence above, emulated in 32-bit arithmetic, equals x / 7 (C's truncating division) for the ends of the range, both sides of the multiples of d near 0 and near the top, and 2^20 random inputs (1073152 inputs)
> magic 7 i32
x / 7 for signed x: i32
  m = ceil(2^34 / 7) = 2454267027 (0x92492493, 32 bits), shift s = 3
  q = floor(x * m / 2^34) for x >= 0, plus 1 for x < 0
  proof (Granlund-Montgomery): m*7 = 2^34 + e with e = 5 <= 2^3.  Write x = q*7 + r, 0 <= r < 7.  Then x*m/2^34 = x/7 + x*e/(7*2^34), and x*e/2^34 <= 1 because |x| <= 2^31, so the extra term is at most 1/7: it cannot carry r/7 <= 6/7 past the next integer
  (for x < 0 the floor lands one below the truncated quotient, since e > 0: hence the + 1)
  as i32, m = -1840700269 (negative: the sequence adds x back):
    C:    t = mulhs(x, -1840700269) + x; q = (t >> 2) + ((uint32_t)x >> 31);   (mulhs: the high 32 bits of the signed product, which already divides by 2^32)
  check: the sequence above, emulated in 32-bit arithmetic, equals x / 7 (C's truncating division) for the ends of the range, both sides of the multiples of d near 0 and near the top, and 2^20 random inputs (1073152 inputs)
> magic 10 u64
x / 10 for unsigned x: u64
  m = ceil(2^67 / 10) = 14757395258967641293 (0xcccccccccccccccd, 64 bits), shift s = 3
  q = floor(x * m / 2^67)
  proof (Granlund-Montgomery): m*10 = 2^67 + e with e = 2 <= 2^3.  Write x = q*10 + r, 0 <= r < 10.  Then x*m/2^67 = x/10 + x*e/(10*2^67), and x*e/2^67 < 1 because |x| < 2^64, so the extra term is below 1/10: it cannot carry r/10 <= 9/10 past the next integer
  m fits u64:
    C:    q = (uint64_t)(((uint128_t)x * 0xcccccccccccccccd) >> 67);
  check: the sequence above, emulated in 64-bit arithmetic, equals x / 10 (C's truncating division) for the ends of the range, both sides of the multiples of d near 0 and near the top, and 2^20 random inputs (1073152 inputs)
> magic 7 u8
x / 7 for unsigned x: u8
  m = ceil(2^11 / 7) = 293 (0x125, 9 bits), shift s = 3
  q = floor(x * m / 2^11)
  proof (Granlund-Montgomery): m*7 = 2^11 + e with e = 3 <= 2^3.  Write x = q*7 + r, 0 <= r < 7.  Then x*m/2^11 = x/7 + x*e/(7*2^11), and x*e/2^11 < 1 because |x| < 2^8, so the extra term is below 1/7: it cannot carry r/7 <= 6/7 past the next integer
  m has 9 bits, one more than u8: the usual sequence uses m - 2^8 = 0x25 and an add that cannot overflow:
    C:    t = mulhi(x, 0x25); q = (t + ((x - t) >> 1)) >> 2;
    in ucas: (u8((u64(x) * 0x25) >> 8) + ((x - u8((u64(x) * 0x25) >> 8)) >> 1)) >> 2
  check: the ucas form above, run by ucas's evaluator in u8, equals x / 7 for every x (256 inputs)
  check: the sequence above, emulated in 8-bit arithmetic, equals x / 7 (C's truncating division) for every input (256 inputs)
> ring GF(16)
GF(2^4) = GF(2)[x]/(x^4 + x + 1), 0x13
  16 elements (2^4), characteristic 2, dimension 4 over GF(2)
  modulus: the smallest primitive polynomial of degree 4 over GF(2) (by its coefficients read as a base-2 number; not necessarily the Conway polynomial)
  a field: yes, the modulus is irreducible (Rabin: x^(2^4) = x mod f and gcd(x^(2^(4/r)) - x, f) = 1 for each prime r | 4)
  check: Ben-Or's test agrees; Berlekamp's matrix has nullity 1 (one irreducible factor)
  check by brute force over all 16 x 16 products: every nonzero element has an inverse, no zero divisors, commutative and equal to shift-and-add
  x has order 15: the modulus is primitive
  unit group: cyclic of order 15 = 3 * 5; phi(15) = 8 generators
  subfields: GF(2), GF(2^2), GF(2^4) (GF(2^d) for each d | 4: the a with a^(2^d) = a)
  Frobenius a -> a^2: an automorphism of order 4 fixing GF(2); the Galois group is cyclic of order 4
> ring GF(16) table
  generator g = 0x2 = x
  antilog: g^k for k = 0, 1, ...
    0x1 0x2 0x4 0x8 0x3 0x6 0xc 0xb 0x5 0xa 0x7 0xe 0xf 0xd 0x9
  log: log_g(a) for a = 1, 2, ...
      0   1   4   2   8   5  10   3  14   9   7   6  13  11  12
  check: g^log(a) = a for every nonzero a; a*b = g^(log a + log b mod 15)
> ring GF(2^8, aes)
GF(2^8, aes) = GF(2)[x]/(x^8 + x^4 + x^3 + x + 1), the AES (Rijndael) polynomial 0x11b
  256 elements (2^8), characteristic 2, dimension 8 over GF(2)
  a field: yes, the modulus is irreducible (Rabin: x^(2^8) = x mod f and gcd(x^(2^(8/r)) - x, f) = 1 for each prime r | 8)
  check: Ben-Or's test agrees; Berlekamp's matrix has nullity 1 (one irreducible factor)
  check by brute force over all 256 x 256 products: every nonzero element has an inverse, no zero divisors, commutative and equal to shift-and-add
  x has order 51: the modulus is not primitive; generator 0x03 = x + 1
  unit group: cyclic of order 255 = 3 * 5 * 17; phi(255) = 128 generators
  subfields: GF(2), GF(2^2), GF(2^4), GF(2^8) (GF(2^d) for each d | 8: the a with a^(2^d) = a)
  Frobenius a -> a^2: an automorphism of order 8 fixing GF(2); the Galois group is cyclic of order 8
> gf(2^8, aes) 0x57 * 0x83
  note: integers are elements in integer notation (the coefficients as base-2 digits): 0x57 = x^6 + x^4 + x^2 + x + 1
0x57 * 0x83 = 0xc1 = x^7 + x^6 + 1 in GF(2^8, aes)
  how: carry-less product 0x2b79, reduced mod (x^8 + x^4 + x^3 + x + 1), the AES (Rijndael) polynomial 0x11b
  check: the same with a second multiplication algorithm (shift-and-add, xtime)
> GF(2^8, aes) 0x53
  note: integers are elements in integer notation (the coefficients as base-2 digits): 0x53 = x^6 + x^4 + x + 1
0x53 = x^6 + x^4 + x + 1 in GF(2^8, aes)
  inverse: 0xca = x^7 + x^6 + x^3 + x (a^(q-2))
  check: 0x53 * 0xca = 0x01 (by log tables)
  order 85 (divides 255)
  log base 0x03: 48 (0x03^48 = 0x53)
  minimal polynomial over GF(2): x^8 + x^6 + x^5 + x^4 + x^2 + x + 1 (degree 8: a lies in GF(2^8))
  conjugates a^(2^i): 0x53 0xb5 0xfc 0x16 0x0f 0x55 0xa1 0xf7
  check: the minimal polynomial is irreducible (Rabin) and vanishes at a: 0x00
  trace 0x00, norm 0x01 (to GF(2))
  square root: 0xf7 (a^(2^7))
> inverse 0x53 in GF(2^8, aes)
  note: integers are elements in integer notation (the coefficients as base-2 digits): 0x53 = x^6 + x^4 + x + 1
0x53^-1 = 0xca = x^7 + x^6 + x^3 + x in GF(2^8, aes)
  how: a^(q-2) (a^(q-1) = 1 for every nonzero a)
  check: 0x53 * 0xca = 0x01 (shift-and-add multiplication)
> log 0x53 in GF(2^8, aes)
  note: integers are elements in integer notation (the coefficients as base-2 digits): 0x53 = x^6 + x^4 + x + 1
  note: no base given: the generator 0x03 (base 2 or the smallest primitive root)
0x03^x = 0x53 in GF(2^8, aes): x = 48 (mod 255, the order of 0x03)
  how: Pohlig-Hellman over 255 = 3 * 5 * 17, baby-step giant-step for each prime
  check: 0x03^48 = 0x53
